    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We have examined scaling relationships of thermal fractures in basalt during characterization for waste remediation at the Idaho National Engineering Laboratory (INEL). These relationships may be of general interest to those who are concerned with fluid flow through volcanic or intrusive rocks where thermal processes are responsible for fracturing. In order to estimate the fracture-controlled hydrology, the first step is to understand the geometry of the fracture system, which is the subject of this contribution.\r\n\r\nThe subsurface of the Snake River Plain is composed largely of basalt flows of various thickness, each containing a network of thermal fractures providing one of the pathways for fluid flow. We measured the overall geometry of 18 basalt flows at a site called Box Canyon, near the INEL, and measured thermal fracture spacing in 7 of these basalt bodies. Based on the widely accepted premise that thermal fracture spacing in basalt flows is related to the local cooling rate, we developed a thermal model to approximate the thermal conditions during fracturing. The field measurements were collected so as to determine the fracture spacing and its variation and to correlate the measured fracture spacing with the calculated cooling rate. In this report, we discuss some of the scaling relationships that appear among the geometry of the basalt flow and the fracture characteristics. Next, we discuss the scaling between the fracture spacing and cooling rates calculated from thermal modeling. Our goal is to link the results of the thermal model to the measured fracture data in such a way as to allow predictions to be made concerning the fracture characteristics in basalt flows in the subsurface. It follows that a good understanding of the thermomechanics of the cooling and fracturing is central to upscaling data and identifying the physical limitations for this procedure.",
